arm64: dts: mt8183: throttle the GPU on the passive trip point

The gpu-thermal zone declares a passive trip at 85C but nothing is bound to
it: the zone has no cooling-maps and the GPU node has no #cooling-cells, so
the passive trip does nothing and the zone heats on to the 100C critical
trip, where the thermal core kills the machine. Panfrost registers a devfreq
cooling device, so binding it to the passive trip is all that is needed to
get the GPU downclocked instead. This matters on the fanless Chromebooks.

Not upstream: MT8183 has carried this gap since its thermal zones were
added. postmarketOS patches the same node (pmaports
device/community/linux-postmarketos-mediatek-mt8183,
arm64-dts-mediatek-mt8183-Add-missing-GPU-clocks.patch) and also rewrites
the GPU clocks; that half is deliberately left out. It belongs to an older
binding - the mediatek,mt8183b-mali node used here takes exactly one clock
plus three power domains, and panfrost understands only core and bus clocks.

--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8183.dtsi
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/mediatek/mt8183.dtsi
@@ -1656,6 +1656,8 @@
 				<&spm MT8183_POWER_DOMAIN_MFG_2D>;
 			power-domain-names = "core0", "core1", "core2";
 
+			#cooling-cells = <2>;
+
 			operating-points-v2 = <&gpu_opp_table>;
 		};
 
@@ -2422,6 +2424,15 @@
 					type = "critical";
 				};
 			};
+
+			cooling-maps {
+				map0 {
+					trip = <&gpu_alert>;
+					cooling-device = <&gpu
+						THERMAL_NO_LIMIT
+						THERMAL_NO_LIMIT>;
+				};
+			};
 		};
 
 		tzts3: md1-thermal {
